From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ysfaculty <haejin.cho@yonsei.ac.kr>
Date: Fri, 16 Oct 2026 22:18:52 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 058/147] hjviewer: carry access tickets to hbbs (staff-only
 gate)

From 1.8.0 on HJViewer is for Yonsei University staff (docs/access-tickets.md
of the hjviewer repository): hjv-api signs short-lived access tickets, and
hbbs (server patch 0006) checks them offline at the punch hole request, the
relay request and the registration. This is the app's plumbing for that; the
screens come next. The app never reads a ticket: it carries the string hjv-api
gave it and asks for the next one by the `exp` and `server_time` beside it.

Controller (src/hjviewer_access.rs, hooked into Client::start):
- PunchHoleRequest.token and RequestRelay.token to hbbs carry the access
  password's ticket for the target (memory only, filled by the next wave's
  POST /api/hjv/guest) or else the login's ticket. Client::start reads it for
  every attempt, so reconnections and each local connection of a port forward
  take the current one. The RequestRelay to hbbr still carries none.
- The login's ticket comes with the login answer (hjv.ticket) and from
  POST /api/hjv/ticket, signed by the login as door 1's requests are (its
  send_by_login, now shared). A desktop app adds its ID and device key, signed
  with that key over the login's token, for a ticket that also registers it.
  A thread asks again once the ticket is past half its lifetime; a logged-in
  app without a ticket that holds asks before connecting (5 s at most). All
  requests share one limiter (10 s apart, backing off to 30 min). The ticket
  lives in LocalConfig and goes at the logout.
- A refusal for access (its text has "access not allowed") makes a logged-in
  app fetch a new ticket once and try again. A refusal that stays reaches the
  screens as msgbox type hjv-access-error with the app's own text (A1 without
  a login, A2 with one, both ending with the mark, which keeps the session
  from reconnecting by itself); screens without the new dialog show it as an
  error box.

PC:
- RegisterPk.hjv_ticket (field 1001, hbb_common patch 0003) carries a bound
  PC's ticket from its sync answer (kept by the service, also in
  HJViewer_ticket.json in its config folder for a restart while hjv-api is
  away; a PC that is not bound keeps none), else the access password's PC
  ticket (memory only, next wave), else the login's ticket of an app that is
  not installed.
- The mediator registers again at once when a new ticket comes after a
  refusal (past NOT_DEPLOYED's 30 s wait) and when the registered one is past
  half its lifetime. A PC that registered with a ticket and has none left (a
  logout, the binding gone, the ticket over) connects again, since a
  RegisterPk without a ticket does not end the registration hbbs took.
- Whether hbbs took the registration is the option hjv-access-pc ("ok",
  "refused") of the process that registers, for the screens.

Pinned builds also pin direct IP access and LAN discovery off: a direct
connection would go around hbbs, and the discovery answer tells anyone on the
network the ID, host name and user name.

What does not change: a build that is not an account build carries nothing,
and an app without a ticket sends an empty token and a RegisterPk that is byte
for byte 1.7.0's, which servers that check nothing (MODE=off, or hbbs before
0006) take as before. Under WebSocket, which every pinned build uses, the
non-empty token's upstream side effects are void: secure_tcp returns at once
and the UDP NAT test does not run under the forced relay.

E2E: --hjv-e2e-ticket [refresh] shows the login's ticket by the first bytes of
its hash, after one request when asked; an access refusal ends
--hjv-e2e-screenshot, -stream and -forward with exit code 5.

Tests: the RegisterPk bytes of the shared vectors with and without a ticket,
an older parser keeping field 1001 as the unknown field hbbs reads, the relay
request carrying the ticket to hbbs and none to hbbr, which ticket is chosen
when, when the PC registers again, the refusal mark, the request's device
signature, and the access password's tickets staying in memory.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
